<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Covenant Chain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1677">1677</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Covenant Chain was a series of alliances between the Iroquois Confederacy and the English colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, formalised at Albany in 1677. The colonies dealt with many other Native nations through the Iroquois, who claimed to speak for them, and the alliance was renewed at regular councils. It underpinned Anglo-Iroquois cooperation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> divided the league.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Covenant Chain grew out of agreements made early in the 17th century between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers in New Netherland and the Five Nations of the Haudenosaunee, which centred on trade. When the English took New Netherland in 1664 and established the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, they renewed them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-philips-war">King Philip's War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, in which more than 600 colonists and 3,000 Indians died, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacons-rebellion">Bacon's Rebellion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> led New York's governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Sir Edmund Andros</a> to ask the Haudenosaunee in August 1675 for help in ending the conflicts; he worked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> leader Daniel Garacontié.</p><p>The name came from the image of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> chain tying the English sailing ship to the Haudenosaunee Tree of Peace in the Onondaga Nation. A three-link silver chain was made for the first agreement, its links standing for peace, friendship and respect between the Haudenosaunee and the Crown. Virginia's governor Lord Effingham used the phrase covenant chain at a council in 1684, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> speaker answered that the chain should be kept clean and bright as silver.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A treaty of 1676 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> and the colonies of Massachusetts Bay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-philips-war">King Philip's War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, the Mohawk having denied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metacom">Metacom</a> gunpowder and attacked his winter camp. A treaty of 1677 brought the Five Nations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> to peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a> allies, many of whom then settled among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>. The Chesapeake had nearly eighty years of peace. In the historian Daniel Richter's words, &quot;Iroquois and New Yorkers played dominant but seldom dictatorial roles&quot; in keeping the treaties.</p><p>Colonial officials later assumed that the treaties had placed the Haudenosaunee under the English sovereign, which the Haudenosaunee rejected; at a council in 1692 their leaders said they would be not like father and son but like brothers. In 1753 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>, saying they had been cheated of their lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, declared the chain broken. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albany-congress">Albany Congress</a> failed to repair it, and the British government set up the British Indian Department in 1755. In a council that year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-johnson">Sir William Johnson</a> renewed the chain, promising yearly meetings to strengthen and brighten it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In June 2010 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> renewed the Covenant Chain Treaties, presenting eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> hand bells each to the chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyendinaga-mohawk-territory">Tyendinaga Mohawk Territory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River</a> to mark 300 years of the chain. The bells were inscribed The Silver Chain of Friendship 1710–2010, a name often used when the chain was renewed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Covenant Chain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the <b>Covenant Chain</b>, the alliance made at Albany in 1677 with Governor Edmund Andros of New York and later extended to other colonies, they became the principal Native partner of the English and claimed authority over many dependent peoples, including the Delaware and remnant groups of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the mid-1670s they were allied with the English in the <b>Covenant Chain</b>, and in 1701 they made the Great Peace of Montreal with the French.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
